OpenSSL 4.1.0: the TLS 1.2 AES-GCM nonce_explicit starts at a random value, not the record sequence number

Kuicksilver's own code, the twelfth patch carried under ADR-0019 (docs/adr/0019-openssl-4-and-ech.md), applied after
openssl-4.1.0-crl-dp-dos.patch; pinned by its SHA-256 in gradle/openssl.toml as patch_12, applied by
tools/openssl/apply-patch.pl to the verified tarball, and dropped with the first OpenSSL release that fixes it (an
upstream issue is drafted, not filed).

The gap: RFC 9325 section 7.2.1 (RECOMMENDED): "TLS 1.2 implementations use the 64-bit sequence number to populate the
nonce_explicit part of the GCM nonce" (RFC 5288 section 3 allows any value that is unique per key). The TLS 1.2 record
layer (ssl/record/methods/tls1_meth.c, tls1_set_crypto_state) gives the AES-GCM context only the 4-byte fixed IV
(EVP_CTRL_GCM_SET_IV_FIXED with its length), and the provider fills the 8-byte invocation field with RAND_bytes
(providers/implementations/ciphers/ciphercommon_gcm.c, gcm_tls_iv_set_fixed), then increments it per record: unique, but
random-based rather than the sequence number, and visible as such on the wire.

The fix: pass the whole 12-byte IV, the fixed part followed by an invocation field of zero, with the length -1 that
gcm_tls_iv_set_fixed already takes for "restore the whole IV" (no randomness drawn). The provider increments the counter
once per record it encrypts (getivgen), from 0 for the first record under the key, which is the record sequence number
of TLS 1.2 (no renegotiation in ksl, and every key change restarts both). Decryption reads the explicit nonce from each
record and is unchanged. ChaCha20-Poly1305 needs nothing: its TLS 1.2 nonce is already the fixed IV XORed with the
sequence number from the AAD (RFC 7905 section 2; cipher_chacha20_poly1305_hw.c, chacha_poly1305_tls_init).

--- a/ssl/record/methods/tls1_meth.c
+++ b/ssl/record/methods/tls1_meth.c
@@ -100,9 +100,25 @@
     }
 
     if (EVP_CIPHER_get_mode(ciph) == EVP_CIPH_GCM_MODE) {
+        /*
+         * RFC 9325, section 7.2.1: the nonce_explicit is the 64-bit record
+         * sequence number. The whole 12-byte IV is set (length -1): the
+         * fixed part, then an invocation field of zero, which the cipher
+         * increments once per record, so in TLS it equals the sequence
+         * number of every record under this key (a random start does not);
+         * in DTLS it stays unique per key.
+         */
+        unsigned char gcm_iv[EVP_GCM_TLS_FIXED_IV_LEN
+            + EVP_GCM_TLS_EXPLICIT_IV_LEN] = { 0 };
+
+        if (ivlen != EVP_GCM_TLS_FIXED_IV_LEN) {
+            ERR_raise(ERR_LIB_SSL, ERR_R_INTERNAL_ERROR);
+            return OSSL_RECORD_RETURN_FATAL;
+        }
+        memcpy(gcm_iv, iv, EVP_GCM_TLS_FIXED_IV_LEN);
         if (!EVP_CipherInit_ex(ciph_ctx, ciph, NULL, key, NULL, enc)
             || EVP_CIPHER_CTX_ctrl(ciph_ctx, EVP_CTRL_GCM_SET_IV_FIXED,
-                   (int)ivlen, iv)
+                   -1, gcm_iv)
                 <= 0) {
             ERR_raise(ERR_LIB_SSL, ERR_R_INTERNAL_ERROR);
             return OSSL_RECORD_RETURN_FATAL;
